WEBVTT

NOTE week13-clip3-teaming
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.578 line:2 align:center
Put a clinician and a model together
and there are two obvious rules:

2
00:00:05.578 --> 00:00:08.946 line:2 align:center
flag if either flags,
or flag only if both do.

3
00:00:08.946 --> 00:00:14.696 line:2 align:center
Say the clinician catches 80%
of cases and the model 75.

4
00:00:14.696 --> 00:00:20.350 line:2 align:center
Which rule is better depends
on something nobody measures.

5
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:26.847 line:2 align:center
Assume their errors are independent.
Of a hundred real cases, both catch 60,

6
00:00:26.847 --> 00:00:33.434 line:2 align:center
the clinician alone 20, the model
alone 15, and 5 are missed by both.

7
00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:40.283 line:2 align:center
Either-flags reaches 0.95,
and both-flags falls to 0.60.

8
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:46.719 line:2 align:center
Now assume the model misses whatever
the clinician misses. Both catch 75,

9
00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:51.528 line:2 align:center
the clinician alone 5,
and 20 are missed by both.

10
00:00:51.528 --> 00:00:56.885 line:2 align:center
Either-flags falls to 0.80,
which is the clinician alone,

11
00:00:56.885 --> 00:01:04.076 line:2 align:center
so the model adds nothing. Both-flags
rises to 0.75. The same two sensitivities,

12
00:01:04.076 --> 00:01:06.350 line:2 align:center
opposite conclusions.

13
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.673 line:2 align:center
And people are not rules.

14
00:01:08.673 --> 00:01:13.241 line:2 align:center
A confident score can talk a
clinician out of a correct judgement,

15
00:01:13.241 --> 00:01:19.125 line:2 align:center
and at one true case in every eight
alerts the rational response is

16
00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:23.300 line:2 align:center
to stop reading them. Goh and
colleagues measured exactly this:

17
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:30.479 line:2 align:center
the model alone scored 92%, and
the physicians using it scored 76,

18
00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:33.417 line:2 align:center
against 74 without.

19
00:01:33.617 --> 00:01:39.176 line:2 align:center
Ambient intelligence watches the room
instead of waiting for someone to type.

20
00:01:39.176 --> 00:01:44.356 line:2 align:center
16 depth sensors detected hand
hygiene at 92.1% sensitivity,

21
00:01:44.356 --> 00:01:51.184 line:2 align:center
better than the human observers at
85.2. But that came from only 88 events,

22
00:01:51.184 --> 00:01:57.027 line:2 align:center
so read the interval. In an ICU, 7
sensors detected mobility at 87%,

23
00:01:57.027 --> 00:02:02.817 line:2 align:center
and counted the staff involved
correctly only 69% of the time.

24
00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:06.627 line:2 align:center
Sensing a care space is a
governance question first.

25
00:02:06.627 --> 00:02:10.935 line:2 align:center
Depth sensing keeps identifiable
images out of the room,

26
00:02:10.935 --> 00:02:16.014 line:2 align:center
which helps, but it does not settle
who consents, who sees the data,

27
00:02:16.014 --> 00:02:21.920 line:2 align:center
or how long it is kept, and the staff
are being observed too. Validate locally,

28
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.713 line:2 align:center
watch it after go-live, and measure
the team rather than the model.
